<section id="commission">
<h2>How the Commission System Works</h2>

<h3>The Two-Tier Structure</h3>
<p>The OLSP System uses a two-tier commission model — a feature that repeatedly prompts the "is this MLM?" question in public discussions. Members earn:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Tier 1 — Direct referrals:</strong> when someone clicks your link and buys an upgrade, you earn a commission on that purchase.</li>
<li><strong>Tier 2 — Downline referrals:</strong> when someone who joined through your link earns their own referral commissions, you may also earn a smaller percentage from those secondary purchases.</li>
</ol>

<p>This downline structure is the part of the OLSP System that most closely resembles multi-level marketing or network-marketing compensation plans. Our dedicated analysis — <a href="/is-olsp-academy-an-mlm/">Is OLSP Academy an MLM?</a> — examines this in detail, including how it compares to regulated MLM models.</p>

<h3>What Public Sources Claim You Can Earn</h3>
<p>Third-party materials citing OLSP commission figures suggest:</p>
<ul>
<li>Free-tier members: modest commission rates, often cited around 10% on certain products.</li>
<li>VIP-tier members: higher rates and flat one-time payouts, with $150–$300 cited for VIP upgrade referrals.</li>
<li>Recurring income: some tools reportedly offer recurring monthly commissions on active subscriptions.</li>
</ul>

<Callout type="warn">
<strong>Income claim caution:</strong> These figures come from sources with a financial interest in promoting sign-ups through their own referral links. They are self-reported, not independently audited, and likely reflect a small, vocal subset of active promoters — not typical member results. We found no independently verified, audited earnings data for any OLSP product tier.
</Callout>
</section>

<section id="experience">
<h2>User Experience &amp; Learning Curve</h2>

<h3>Getting Started</h3>
<p>Public walkthroughs consistently describe a short sign-up process: create an account, complete the Bootcamp training modules, receive a personal Mega Link, and start sharing it. The system is explicitly designed for people with "zero tech skills," and sources agree that the initial setup requires no technical knowledge, website building, or audience building.</p>

<h3>The Daily Routine</h3>
<p>Ongoing activity centers on driving traffic to the Mega Link — through social media posts, content creation, paid advertisements, or OLSP's own traffic tools — and attending or rewatching the live training sessions. The member's primary job is generating clicks; the system handles the follow-up sales process.</p>

<h3>The Structural Trade-Off</h3>
<p>The convenience of a done-for-you funnel comes with a trade-off: members are entirely dependent on the OLSP platform for their income. If OLSP changes its compensation plan, retires a tool, or discontinues a service, members who built their approach around it lose the system they were trained to depend on. This is a structural risk shared by all single-vendor "earn inside our ecosystem" platforms, but it is especially relevant here because of the number of separate paid add-ons involved.</p>
</section>

